The short launcher replaces repeated bootstrap code only because the web starter and auto-configuration conditions supply the matching infrastructure.

```java
@Configuration
class DbConfig {
    @Bean
    DataSource dataSource() {
        var dataSource = new HikariDataSource();
        dataSource.setJdbcUrl("jdbc:postgresql://localhost/orders");
        return dataSource;
    }
}
```

When the relevant auto-configuration uses `@ConditionalOnMissingBean`, this application bean makes the default back off. That can be intentional, but the application now owns the missing settings and lifecycle details.

```java
@Service
class OrderService {
    private final OrderRepository repository;

    OrderService(OrderRepository repository) {
        this.repository = repository;
    }

    @Transactional                         // Spring Framework transaction support
    Order create(String item) {
        return repository.save(new Order(null, item));
    }
}

interface OrderRepository extends JpaRepository<Order, Long> {} // Spring Data JPA
```

With the JPA starter, driver, and connection properties in place, Boot can supply common `DataSource` and JPA infrastructure. The service and transaction model still use their owning project APIs.

